.Dd October 4, 2026
.Dt MARQUEE 1
.Os marquee 0.1.0
.Sh NAME
.Nm marquee
.Nd scroll text across the terminal, measured in display columns
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bk -words
.Nm marquee
.Op Fl bounce | Fl continuous
.Op Fl direction Ar left | right
.Op Fl gap Ar COLUMNS
.Op Fl speed Ar MS | Fl fps Ar FPS
.Op Fl once | Fl repeat Ar N
.Op Fl align Ar left | center | right
.Op Fl no-color | Fl theme Ar NAME | Fl theme-file Ar PATH
.Op Fl big
.Op Fl scale Ar N
.Op Fl font Ar name
.Op Fl h | Fl help
.Op Fl V | Fl version
.Op Ar TEXT
.Ek
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
scrolls
.Ar TEXT
across the terminal on the single line the cursor is on: the text enters at one
edge, travels one display column per frame, leaves at the other edge, and
starts again after
.Fl gap
blank columns.
By default the next cycle waits for the text to leave the screen entirely;
with
.Fl continuous
the next copy of the text follows the previous one, the
.Fl gap
columns behind it, so the screen never empties once the first copy has
entered \(em the classic LED-marquee stream.
It loops forever until it has run the cycles it was asked for or it is stopped
with Ctrl+C.
.Pp
Widths are counted the way terminals count them:
CJK characters occupy 2 columns,
emoji occupy 2,
combining marks occupy 0,
and a family joined by zero-width joiners is one unit of 2 columns.
Text is segmented into grapheme clusters, never into code points, and a cluster
that would straddle the edge of a frame is dropped for that frame rather than
split \(em a terminal cannot draw half a character.
Every frame is exactly the terminal's width in columns, so nothing shifts and
nothing wraps.
.Pp
.Nm
takes over the current line only: the cursor is hidden, no alternate screen is
entered, and the scrollback is left intact.
On every way out \(em the cycles ran out, Ctrl+C, an I/O error, even a panic
\(em the line is erased, the cursor is shown again and raw mode is left behind,
so the shell prompt lands on a clean line.
.Pp
When the terminal is resized mid-scroll, the next frame is drawn at the new
width with the text kept at the same point of its trip.
.Sh BIG FONT MODE
With
.Fl big ,
the text is not drawn as terminal characters but as large pixel glyphs:
each grapheme cluster maps to one glyph of the embedded Fusion Pixel Font
(12px, Simplified Chinese coverage plus ASCII, a fallback box for anything
missing), and each font pixel becomes an N\(muN block of terminal cells drawn
with half-block characters when
.Nm
runs at
.Fl scale Ar N .
The glyphs span 6\(muN rows: the line the cursor is on plus the rows above
it.
See
.Sx OPTIONS
for the flags and
.Sx TERMINAL BEHAVIOUR
for what stays the same.
.Pp
The scrolling model is unchanged: one column of travel per frame, the same
direction, bounce, gap and cycle options, a frame exactly the terminal's
width, and an edge that cuts through a glyph clips it by whole cells so the
motion never jumps a column.
.Pp
.Fl scale
names the terminal it needs: at least 12\(muN columns (one whole glyph) by
6\(muN+1 rows (the glyph rows plus one line of margin). A terminal smaller
than that is refused before the first frame; one that shrinks below it
mid-run is reported once on standard error while the scroll carries on.
See
.Sx TERMINAL BEHAVIOUR .
.Pp
Like the single line, no alternate screen is entered and a frame is one
buffered write; on every way out \(em the cycles ran out, Ctrl+C, an I/O
error, a panic \(em the rows the marquee occupied are erased and the shell's
line comes back blank.
.Fl align
has no effect in big mode.
.Sh INPUT
.Ar TEXT
may be given as a positional argument or piped on standard input.
When both are possible the rule is, in order:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
A positional
.Ar TEXT
wins, and standard input is never read.
.It
With no
.Ar TEXT ,
piped standard input is read to EOF: each line is trimmed, blank lines are
dropped, and the rest are joined with a single space, because a marquee scrolls
one line.
.It
With no
.Ar TEXT
and a terminal on standard input,
.Nm
refuses to block waiting for you to type: it exits 2 with a message instead.
.El
.Pp
Blank input is refused either way (exit 2), since there would be nothing to
scroll.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width 14n
.It Ar TEXT
The text to scroll.
If omitted, standard input is read according to the rule above.
.It Fl speed Ar MS
Milliseconds per column step: 1 to 10000.
Default 50, i.e. 20 column steps per second.
Cannot be combined with
.Fl fps .
.It Fl fps Ar FPS
Frames per second: 1 to 1000, where one frame is one column step.
Cannot be combined with
.Fl speed .
When neither is given the default of one step every 50 ms applies.
.It Fl direction Ar direction
Which edge the text enters from:
.Cm left
(default) enters at the right edge and leaves at the left, the classic marquee;
.Cm right
mirrors it.
.It Fl bounce
Reverse at both edges instead of wrapping around to re-enter.
A bounce cycle is the trip to one edge, the gap, the trip back, and the gap
again.
Cannot be combined with
.Fl continuous :
a bounce reverses at the edges, a continuous stream follows itself around
them.
.It Fl continuous
Start the next copy of the text before the previous one has left the screen,
.Fl gap
columns behind it.
One cycle is then one period of the stream \(em the text plus its gap \(em so
.Fl once
and
.Fl repeat
count passes of the content past any fixed column, and the screen does not
end blank when the cycles run out.
A gap wider than the viewport still shows blank frames between the copies.
.It Fl gap Ar COLUMNS
Blank columns between the end of one cycle and the start of the next, or
between consecutive copies with
.Fl continuous .
Default 8.
.It Fl once
Scroll exactly one cycle, then exit with status 0.
The same as
.Fl repeat Ar 1 .
Cannot be combined with
.Fl repeat .
.It Fl repeat Ar N
Scroll exactly
.Ar N
cycles (N \(>= 1), then exit with status 0.
Cannot be combined with
.Fl once .
.It Fl align Ar alignment
Where text shorter than the terminal would sit while it is on screen:
.Cm left ,
.Cm center
or
.Cm right ,
default
.Cm left .
.Pp
Currently accepted and validated but
.Em not implemented :
text scrolls from the edge as usual, regardless of this flag.
It is reserved for a static placement mode.
.It Fl no-color
Never emit colour escape sequences, whatever
.Fl theme
says; a non-empty
.Ev NO_COLOR
in the environment does the same.
.Pp
The theme name is still validated: a typo is reported even when colour is off.
.It Fl theme Ar name
Which theme the text is painted with, on the single line and in big mode
alike: the theme's foreground colours the text (the half-blocks in big mode),
its background the blanks.
.Cm default
(the default) is plain; the other built-ins are
.Cm bold ,
.Cm alarm ,
.Cm gold ,
.Cm matrix ,
.Cm ice ,
.Cm violet ,
and the palettes
.Cm rainbow
(six vivid ANSI colours, one per column),
.Cm sunset ,
.Cm ocean
and
.Cm neon .
.Pp
A palette is banded: its colours cycle across the screen's display columns
and the text flows through them; a glyph wider than
.Ql band
columns shows several colours at once.
.Pp
A theme file adds or overrides themes by name; see
.Fl theme-file .
An unknown name is a usage error that lists what is available.
.It Fl theme-file Ar path
Read user themes from this file instead of the default location.
The file is a small TOML subset: one
.Ql [name]
section per theme, with
.Ql fg
and
.Ql bg
as a colour \(em one of the 16 ANSI colour names such as
.Ql red
or
.Ql bright-red ,
or
.Ql #rrggbb ,
or a list of colours, a palette
\(em and
.Ql bold ,
.Ql dim ,
.Ql italic
and
.Ql underline
as booleans, plus
.Ql band
as the number of columns each palette colour spans (default 1).
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[alert]
fg = "bright-red"
bold = true

[ocean]
fg = "#7fd4ff"
bg = "blue"
dim = true

[sunset]
fg = ["#ff5e62", "#ff9966", "#ffd194"]
band = 4
.Ed
.Pp
Without
.Fl theme-file ,
themes come from
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/marquee/themes.toml
or
.Pa ~/.config/marquee/themes.toml
when that file exists; a file given explicitly must exist and parse, and a
malformed line is reported with its line number.
.It Fl big
Scroll the text as big pixel glyphs spanning several terminal rows, rendered
from the embedded Fusion Pixel Font with half-block characters.
See
.Sx BIG FONT MODE .
.It Fl scale Ar N
Pixel magnification for
.Fl big :
1 to 32, default 1.
The text then spans 6\(muN terminal rows.
The terminal must be at least 12\(muN columns by 6\(muN+1 rows; anything
smaller is refused before the first frame (exit 2).
Requires
.Fl big .
.It Fl font Ar name
Which packaged pixel font
.Fl big
renders with:
.Cm zh-hans
(default) is the Simplified Chinese atlas.
Other variants are planned but not packaged yet, and naming one is a usage
error.
Requires
.Fl big .
.It Fl h , Fl help
Print help; the long form prints every option in full.
.It Fl V , Fl version
Print version and exit.
.El
.Sh TERMINAL BEHAVIOUR
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Ctrl+C
The only way to stop an unbounded scroll.
It is read as input in raw mode, exits 0, and leaves the terminal restored.
.It Resize
A resize is observed while running, not only at startup: the very next frame is
drawn at the new width, with the text kept at the same point of its trip.
In big-font mode the scale is re-fitted to the new height for the same frame,
down when the window shrinks and back toward the request when it grows again.
A window that shrinks below the big-font size limits never stops the scroll:
the run degrades and one notice naming the size it needs goes to standard
error.
A zero-width report (a window that is hidden or still being laid out) is
ignored in favour of the last real width.
.It Redirected output
When standard output is not a terminal, each frame is written as one plain
line: no cursor positioning, no escape bytes.
In big-font mode a frame is 6\(muN such lines, one per cell row.
The frames are paced as usual.
When the reader of the pipe goes away (piping
.Nm
into
.Xr head 1 ,
for example),
.Nm
exits 0 in silence rather than reporting a broken pipe.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std marquee
.Bl -tag -width 5n
.It 0
The cycles ran out, the scroll was stopped with Ctrl+C, or the reader of
redirected output went away.
.It 1
Drawing failed: a real I/O error.
.It 2
Usage: no text, blank text, a bad flag (the command-line parser's own error
code), a theme that cannot be resolved \(em an unknown name, or a theme
file that is missing or malformed \(em or a terminal smaller than
big-font mode needs.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Ev COLUMNS
The width assumed when standard output is not a terminal (pipe, file, CI) and
this holds a sane positive number.
Otherwise 80.
.It Ev LINES
The height assumed in the same situation.
Otherwise 24.
.It Ev NO_COLOR
When set to a non-empty value, marquee scrolls plain regardless of
.Fl theme ,
the https://no-color.org convention.
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME , Ev HOME
Where the default theme file lives:
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/marquee/themes.toml ,
or
.Pa $HOME/.config/marquee/themes.toml
when XDG is unset.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Scroll a mixed CJK and emoji string at the default speed:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ marquee \(dq你好世界 · Hello Terminal 🚀\(dq
.Ed
.Pp
One cycle, slower (120 ms per column), then exit:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ marquee --speed 120 --once \(dq deploying... \(dq
.Ed
.Pp
30 frames per second, entering from the left edge:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ marquee --fps 30 --direction right \(dq slow news day \(dq
.Ed
.Pp
Bounce between the edges, a small gap, exactly three cycles:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ marquee --bounce --gap 2 --repeat 3 \(dq ping \(dq
.Ed
.Pp
Scroll text from a pipe; a positional argument would win over it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ echo \(dq正在部署...\(dq | marquee
$ git log --oneline -1 | marquee --speed 30
.Ed
.Pp
Big pixel glyphs at double size, spanning twelve rows:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ marquee \(dq你好世界\(dq --big --scale 2
.Ed
.Pp
Redirected output is one plain line per frame, with no escape bytes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ marquee --once \(dq hi \(dq > frames.txt
.Ed
.Sh KNOWN GAPS
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Fl align
is accepted and validated but has no effect yet (see
.Sx OPTIONS ) .
.It
.Fl theme-file
covers the whole theme grammar; per-mode themes (one look for the single
line, another for big mode) are not supported yet.
.El
